A||||12||FieroPerformance||New clutch, not fully engaged, can it be adjusted?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||12-01-2001||08:29 PM||||I was on the highway for the first time today with my newly rebuilt engine and new clutch kit. While in 5th gear running about 80 mph I floored it and the RPMs went up but the car did not accelerate. When I put the slave cylinder back on it seemed like it was pushing rather hard on the clutch release lever. My question is this, if in fact the reason for the slipage is that the fork is pushing the throwout bearing up against my pressure plate, would putting some washers behind the bolts that hold on the slave cylinder solve my problem? Or shortening the rod that comes out of the cylinder?<P>Anyone have this problem with a new clutch? <P>I have the Isuzu 5 speed on my GT. I put in a heavy duty clutch kit.<P>Thanks,<BR>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroPerformance||12-01-2001||09:03 PM||||come on you guys, I am sitting here waiting to go out in the garage and try to fix it.<P>*BUMP*||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||12-01-2001||09:41 PM||||a new HD clutch usually requires a breakin period, about 500 miles of easy driving. Not uncommon for them to slip for the first few miles- take it easy for a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-01-2001||10:27 PM||||Here's something I did on my '88 after the clutch started slipping (for reasons that are similar to what you are describing, we think) - we installed about 20 flatwashers on the two posts (10 each) that mount the slave cylinder to the mounting bracket, moving the slave cylinder away from the bracket... as long as you can smoothly get the car in gear (i.e., without grinding or forcing it), then the clutch is still releasing enough to operate properly. <P>If the slave cylinder is pressing against the throwout bearing all the time,  then the clutch will be slipping just a bit and will eventually cause overheating and glazing of the contact surfaces. (At least that's the theory we're operating on at the moment... won't know for sure what the problem was until we get it all back apart again...)||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroPerformance||12-01-2001||10:54 PM||||Thanks for the feedback you guys. <P>This is a great online community.<P>-Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||12-01-2001||11:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B><BR>If the slave cylinder is pressing against the throwout bearing all the time,  then the clutch will be slipping just a bit and will eventually cause overheating and glazing of the contact surfaces. (At least that's the theory we're operating on at the moment... won't know for sure what the problem was until we get it all back apart again...)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Because the slave has a spring in it and there is always slight pressure on the clutch arm, the TO bearing is always in contact with the pressure plate. just a bit of usless info<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroPerformance||12-01-2001||11:57 PM||||Just a though - are the slave cylinders on the 4 cylinder Fieros different than on the V6s? It seems like the rod that pushes the fork is just too long and getting pressed to hard against the fork.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||12-02-2001||01:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroPerformance:<BR><B>Just a though - are the slave cylinders on the 4 cylinder Fieros different than on the V6s? It seems like the rod that pushes the fork is just too long and getting pressed to hard against the fork.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I think that there are three different styles, one for each tranny. So yes it can make a difference.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroMaster88||12-02-2001||02:19 AM||||I have the opposite problem......new clutch, master, slave, line and steel petal...damn thin wont disengage all the way.  Cant even get it into gear at a stop from neutral.  If I put it into first before coming to a stop..it will idle, but at about 600 RPM..... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||12-02-2001||12:35 PM||||Breakin period? on a clutch... Remind me not to buy whatever product that is... <P>If this persists... something is binding or the pressure plate is weak. Or it's one crappy clutch disk.<P>Hydraulic clutches usually keep the throwout on the pressure plate full time. It's what makes them self adjusting. All the ones I've ever worked on do it. I can't imagine Fiero wouldn't. The spring that does this doesn't have enough force to cause the disk to slip.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||12-02-2001||04:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Breakin period? on a clutch... Remind me not to buy whatever product that is... <P>If this persists... something is binding or the pressure plate is weak. Or it's one crappy clutch disk.<P>Hydraulic clutches usually keep the throwout on the pressure plate full time. It's what makes them self adjusting. All the ones I've ever worked on do it. I can't imagine Fiero wouldn't. The spring that does this doesn't have enough force to cause the disk to slip.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Both Centerforce and McLeod include a slip with their products giving the instructions for the proper break-in. The slip is marked in rather large letters -IMPORTANT||||reg||1
Z||000011||LZeitgeist||12-02-2001||06:06 PM||||O.K., let me clarify what I meant up there...<P>If the slave cylinder is pressing against the throwout bearing <B>with enough pressure to partially disengage and release pressure from the clutch disc </B>all the time, then the clutch will be slipping just a bit and will eventually cause overheating and glazing of the contact surfaces.<P>Sorry I wasn't more clear.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>EDIT - typos<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroPerformance||12-02-2001||08:39 PM||||Alright. Today we removed the slave cylinder and cleaned it out (it was dirty inside), after this nothing happened when I pressed the clutch pedal so we removed the master cylinder to find that the spring inside was broken. My two problems now are:<P>1. Replacing the master cylinder for $100 from atuozone or finding a new spring.<P>2. Finding out what is causing my clutch to slip if it wasn't being caused by faulty cylinders.<P>My clutch is a borg-warner and is a 6 spring desing. I have just over 500 miles on the engine and clutch.<P>Please give me any advice that you can.<P>Thanks,<BR>-Charlie||||reg||1
